@* Pagination's Overview, written by hand after the selection Overviews: pages in the address, more
   than one on a page (code), sizes and how many pages show, and bound to C#. Accessibility and See
   also come from the doc comment. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>Each page is a link that sets <Code>?page=</Code> and keeps the rest of the address, so paging works on static pages with JavaScript off, survives a reload and can be shared. Pagination only draws the links: the page reads the number and shows its own rows. A <Anchor Href="docs/components/table">Table</Anchor> pages itself with the same links.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Pages in the address</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>TotalItems</Code> and <Code>PageSize</Code>, or <Code>PageCount</Code>, for how many pages there are, and read the page with <Code>[SupplyParameterFromQuery]</Code> to show its rows.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="pagination-url" />
<TextBlock>Page 1 leaves the key out of the address. Pagination treats a number past the last page as the last, so clamp your own the same way, as the example does; at the ends, previous or next stays in place, marked unavailable.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">More than one on a page</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>QueryKey</Code> to give each pagination on a page its own key in the address, so they page apart, and <Code>Label</Code> to name each one for screen readers.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Several</Code>
<TextBlock>A <Anchor Href="docs/components/filter-form">FilterForm</Anchor> drops a <Code>page</Code> key, or one ending in <Code>-page</Code>, when the filters change, so filtering starts again at page 1.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Sizes and how many pages show</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Size</Code> to match the buttons and fields around it, <Code>Siblings</Code> for the pages on each side of the current one, and <Code>Boundaries</Code> for the pages always shown at each end.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="pagination-sizes" />
<TextBlock>The row keeps the same number of slots as the page moves, so the controls don't jump about. Below 640px only the current page and the ends show, with a gap for each run left out, so it fits a phone.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Bound to C#</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>@@bind-Page</Code> on an interactive page to page in C#: the pages become buttons that update the bound value, and the address stays as it is.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="pagination-bound" />
<TextBlock>A static page has no C# to update, so there the pages stay links that set the key; start the bound value from the address, as the example does with <Code>?p=</Code>, so both work.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Its own words</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Label</Code> for the navigation landmark's name ("Pagination"), <Code>PreviousLabel</Code> and <Code>NextLabel</Code> for the arrows ("Previous page", "Next page"), and <Code>PageLabel</Code> for the word read before each number ("Page").</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        @foreach (var order in Orders.Skip((Page - 1) * 20).Take(20))
        {
            <OrderRow Order="order" />
        }
        <Pagination TotalItems="Orders.Count" PageSize="20" Label="Orders pages" />

        @code {
            [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "page")]
            private int? CurrentPage { get; set; }

            private int PageCount => Math.Max(1, (Orders.Count + 19) / 20);
            private int Page => Math.Clamp(CurrentPage ?? 1, 1, PageCount);
        }
        """;

    private const string Several = """
        <Pagination TotalItems="Orders.Count" QueryKey="orders-page" Label="Orders pages" />
        <Pagination TotalItems="Returns.Count" QueryKey="returns-page" Label="Returns pages" />
        """;
}
